00:05:05.810 --> 00:05:08.623
and it's something I think about so much.

00:05:09.460 --> 00:05:14.360
How underrated is the brain
in our current society

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:17.910
when it comes to genuinely, you know,

00:05:17.910 --> 00:05:20.190
obviously, there's diseases and things

00:05:20.190 --> 00:05:21.830
that are just it's kind of math thing.

00:05:21.830 --> 00:05:25.400
But like I'm fascinated
by what you just said.

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:28.610
Because I'm uncomfortable
in how much I believe it.

00:05:28.610 --> 00:05:31.130
And where I'm going with
that is I just think

00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:33.950
you can will shit into existence.

00:05:33.950 --> 00:05:36.887
- I mean your attitude
is a big, big part of it

00:05:36.887 --> 00:05:38.310
and how us sort of approach it,

00:05:38.310 --> 00:05:41.280
your confidence in seeing
that glass as half full

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:43.250
and really believing
that you can persevere

00:05:43.250 --> 00:05:46.360
and overcome your situation
is a big component of it.

00:05:46.360 --> 00:05:48.520
Now, granted there are medical conditions

00:05:48.520 --> 00:05:50.320
that are challenging and--

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:51.920
- If somebody rips your
heart out of your body

00:05:51.920 --> 00:05:54.390
like Mortal Combat even
though I'd sitting there

00:05:54.390 --> 00:05:55.520
being like I'm not gonna die.

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:57.030
I'm probably gonna die at that point.

00:05:57.030 --> 00:05:57.863
- [Sampson] At that point, right.

00:05:57.863 --> 00:05:59.513
You can see yourself,
you can see your heart

00:05:59.513 --> 00:06:00.715
and you know how it's gonna end, but.

00:06:00.715 --> 00:06:01.800
(he laughs)

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:03.560
But keep in mind--

00:06:03.560 --> 00:06:05.680
- How advanced are the
studies in literally

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:08.247
that person laying there
where they're a 50/50,

00:06:08.247 --> 00:06:10.096
a 70/30 not gonna make it.

00:06:10.096 --> 00:06:12.150
I've never even heard mortality rates.

00:06:12.150 --> 00:06:14.830
So like, you know but
like that 70/30 person

00:06:14.830 --> 00:06:18.170
has there been enough
data on the woman or guy

00:06:18.170 --> 00:06:21.358
that laid there and said,
fuck this I'm gonna win

00:06:21.358 --> 00:06:23.560
and what that variable is versus

00:06:23.560 --> 00:06:27.060
what most people hear is like,
oh, my god 70%, I'm done.

00:06:27.060 --> 00:06:29.360
Has there, is there any advance--

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:30.430
- I mean the studies have shown,

00:06:30.430 --> 00:06:31.790
I don't know any particular study,

00:06:31.790 --> 00:06:33.930
but I do know that studies have shown

00:06:33.930 --> 00:06:35.130
is all about your attitude.

00:06:35.130 --> 00:06:37.530
I mean, of course, the
medical condition is variable.

00:06:37.530 --> 00:06:39.330
So the medical condition
or whatever you have

00:06:39.330 --> 00:06:41.537
and been diagnosed with, the situation

00:06:41.537 --> 00:06:43.120
it's all about your
attitude and your approach

00:06:43.120 --> 00:06:44.050
and your conditioning.

00:06:44.050 --> 00:06:46.860
If you're gonna choose to
exercise and believe and hope

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:49.230
and that you're gonna overcome
and beat this situation.

00:06:49.230 --> 00:06:50.220
Yes, it will happen.

00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:51.060
Now, if you're gonna sit back.

00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:52.609
You're diagnosed with something

00:06:52.609 --> 00:06:55.370
well I'm not gonna take
this medicine, right, then--

00:06:55.370 --> 00:06:57.090
- You're talking about
something super interesting.

00:06:57.090 --> 00:06:58.850
You know like, I don't
know any of the details

00:06:58.850 --> 00:07:00.220
of the Steve Jobs thing.

00:07:00.220 --> 00:07:02.128
- [Sampson] Right, right, right.

00:07:02.128 --> 00:07:03.000
- Like the classic headline reading.

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:04.980
Like I remember thinking, like hearing

00:07:04.980 --> 00:07:07.190
like maybe he did some alternative
stuff right from the get

00:07:07.190 --> 00:07:09.660
and I was like, hmm,
I'm that kind of person.

00:07:09.660 --> 00:07:11.060
So let's make sure I
don't make that kind of,

00:07:11.060 --> 00:07:13.663
like I took note of that to your point.

00:07:14.920 --> 00:07:15.900
You know it's very interesting

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:16.890
we're having this conversation.

00:07:16.890 --> 00:07:18.730
It kind of makes me
think about all the shit

00:07:18.730 --> 00:07:20.080
that I pump out into the world.

00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:22.970
Which is like look, everybody
who's listening right now,

00:07:22.970 --> 00:07:23.953
to make it contextual for you,

00:07:23.953 --> 00:07:28.400
you hae a certain level of
natural entrepreneurial talent.

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:30.060
No different than sports talent.

00:07:30.060 --> 00:07:31.760
No different than anything else.

00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:36.260
But the perspective you
deploy changes the outcome

00:07:36.260 --> 00:07:38.800
to the maximum of what that is.

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:40.570
- And at times, I mean
even with this stuff

00:07:40.570 --> 00:07:43.260
at times it may not be at
where you want it to be

00:07:43.260 --> 00:07:44.093
but it's nurturing.

00:07:44.093 --> 00:07:46.630
It's developing and it's
every day processing

00:07:46.630 --> 00:07:47.570
and putting energy into it.

00:07:47.570 --> 00:07:50.340
And as you do so, then your belief

00:07:50.340 --> 00:07:52.060
and the growth of what you can achieve

00:07:52.060 --> 00:07:54.150
and how you can overcome
and how you can persevere

00:07:54.150 --> 00:07:57.540
whether it's medical,
personal life, professional,

00:07:57.540 --> 00:07:59.593
it starts to develop and take flight.

00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:02.930
- Who were you as a kid?

00:08:02.930 --> 00:08:03.763
- [Sharlee] Me?

00:08:03.763 --> 00:08:04.733
- Yep.

00:08:04.733 --> 00:08:05.690
(she chuckles)

00:08:05.690 --> 00:08:07.393
- I was a tomboy.

00:08:08.500 --> 00:08:11.616
But I was somebody, I was
raised, a very close-knit family.

00:08:11.616 --> 00:08:15.614
Had parents that told us we
could accomplish anything.

00:08:15.614 --> 00:08:17.110
I tell you I was very fortunate

00:08:17.110 --> 00:08:18.520
because they wouldn't let us listen

00:08:18.520 --> 00:08:21.040
to the outside world, you know, and hear.

00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:22.290
But also, too, you know,

00:08:22.290 --> 00:08:25.140
you can only be shielded for so long.

00:08:25.140 --> 00:08:26.530
You have to go out into the real world

00:08:26.530 --> 00:08:29.157
which is what I was hit with
when I went off to college.

00:08:29.157 --> 00:08:31.290
- Did they do a good, you know it's funny

00:08:31.290 --> 00:08:32.370
'cause I saw Andy react

00:08:32.370 --> 00:08:33.713
to like not hearing the outside world.

00:08:33.713 --> 00:08:36.320
It's something I talk
about so much with my mom

00:08:36.320 --> 00:08:38.262
and dad did for me as well.

00:08:38.262 --> 00:08:42.940
Did they do a great job
in practical optimism?

00:08:42.940 --> 00:08:46.200
Like did you have like that
optimism like I can do anything

00:08:46.200 --> 00:08:48.860
but were they great at
not creating delusion?

00:08:48.860 --> 00:08:50.200
- Yes, very good at it.

00:08:50.200 --> 00:08:51.080
- Right, because isn't that,

00:08:51.080 --> 00:08:52.730
is that your perspective on that?

00:08:52.730 --> 00:08:56.280
Like to me, what is so fascinating is

00:08:56.280 --> 00:08:57.810
the current state of parenting,

00:08:57.810 --> 00:09:00.400
the reason we have 8th
place trophies is we took

00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:04.260
you can do anything, but we
created fake environments.

00:09:04.260 --> 00:09:06.430
We eliminated accountability.

00:09:06.430 --> 00:09:08.840
And that's why we're
skewed the other way now.

00:09:08.840 --> 00:09:10.301
And to me it's the balance of,

00:09:10.301 --> 00:09:13.030
I call it practical optimism, right.

00:09:13.030 --> 00:09:16.380
Or just like a limit,
when you tell your kid

00:09:16.380 --> 00:09:19.840
you can do anything I
think where the parent

00:09:19.840 --> 00:09:23.000
really needs to be thoughtful
is when that kid then goes

00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:26.670
and sings at a talent show and sucks shit,

00:09:26.670 --> 00:09:28.735
you can't tell them they're Beyonce.

00:09:28.735 --> 00:09:29.568
(Sampson laughs)

00:09:29.568 --> 00:09:30.649
- [Sharlee] Right.

00:09:30.649 --> 00:09:31.600
- You can't because what
a lot of people are doing

00:09:31.600 --> 00:09:33.612
is you didn't win this talent show

00:09:33.612 --> 00:09:35.620
but it was the judge's fault.

00:09:35.620 --> 00:09:36.650
It was a bad night.

00:09:36.650 --> 00:09:38.800
They like start creating excuses at scale

00:09:38.800 --> 00:09:41.530
and now you have 23 year
olds who enter the world

00:09:41.530 --> 00:09:43.770
full of excuse framework.

00:09:43.770 --> 00:09:46.510
- Well, I mean a really interesting story

00:09:46.510 --> 00:09:50.400
is that an early age
Derek went off to school

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:52.460
and said his dream was to become

00:09:52.460 --> 00:09:53.908
shortstop for the New York Yankees.

00:09:53.908 --> 00:09:54.741
- [Gary] Is that true?

00:09:54.741 --> 00:09:56.950
- He had teachers, this
is 8th grade I believe,

00:09:56.950 --> 00:09:59.270
he had teachers that said to my parents

00:09:59.270 --> 00:10:01.730
that you need to put real
thoughts into his head.

00:10:01.730 --> 00:10:03.615
And my parents turned around and said,

00:10:03.615 --> 00:10:05.640
in our mind he has real thoughts.

00:10:05.640 --> 00:10:06.556
He has to work.

00:10:06.556 --> 00:10:07.389
- [Gary] Was he good at
baseball at that point?

00:10:07.389 --> 00:10:08.290
- No, he wasn't great.

00:10:08.290 --> 00:10:09.123
- [Gary] Really?

00:10:09.123 --> 00:10:11.650
- But it was he has to
work very, very hard

00:10:11.650 --> 00:10:12.680
and they told him that.

00:10:12.680 --> 00:10:14.560
They said, if this is what you wanna do,

00:10:14.560 --> 00:10:17.570
you can accomplish it,
but it is gonna be very,

00:10:17.570 --> 00:10:20.380
very hard for you to get there.

00:10:20.380 --> 00:10:22.080
You're gonna have to
work really, really hard

00:10:22.080 --> 00:10:23.430
and put your all into it.

00:10:23.430 --> 00:10:25.670
So that's where you have to be realistic.

00:10:25.670 --> 00:10:29.295
It is a possibility, but
you're gonna have to work hard.

00:10:29.295 --> 00:10:30.330
- My, and the way he says it,

00:10:30.330 --> 00:10:33.220
my six and three quarters year old son

00:10:33.220 --> 00:10:35.210
is obsessed with basketball.

00:10:35.210 --> 00:10:38.960
And he talks about it, he talks
like me, 24/7 every second.

00:10:38.960 --> 00:10:41.393
After two weeks of vacation just now,

00:10:41.393 --> 00:10:44.880
like my music teacher in first
grade called me motor mouth

00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:47.590
and I'm watching it my face.

00:10:47.590 --> 00:10:50.720
But he already in the last
six months I've got him into

00:10:50.720 --> 00:10:54.993
like he already knows
that he will never dunk.

00:10:56.000 --> 00:10:58.170
Right, as a kid and the
little basket in your house

00:10:58.170 --> 00:10:59.830
you're dunking like crazy.

00:10:59.830 --> 00:11:02.780
And six months ago, I was
like, bro, you will never dunk.

00:11:02.780 --> 00:11:05.240
He cried, he was devastated.

00:11:05.240 --> 00:11:08.110
Like for a week it was
like tormenting him.

00:11:08.110 --> 00:11:12.440
But he is now taking so
many fucking jump shots

00:11:12.440 --> 00:11:14.143
and he's like do you
think I'll be in the NBA?

00:11:14.143 --> 00:11:16.710
And I'm like I don't think so.

00:11:16.710 --> 00:11:18.600
But if you work ridiculously hard

00:11:18.600 --> 00:11:20.588
and miraculously become the first person

00:11:20.588 --> 00:11:23.760
that can hit jump shots from half court.

00:11:23.760 --> 00:11:26.540
Because I think your 5'10",
the likelihood of your life

00:11:26.540 --> 00:11:29.060
is going to be a
challenge on the evolution

00:11:29.060 --> 00:11:32.280
of like everybody being
seven feet in the NBA.

00:11:32.280 --> 00:11:33.790
I just believe in that shit.

00:11:33.790 --> 00:11:35.950
- But on the flip side, Gary.

00:11:35.950 --> 00:11:40.630
Is there an opportunity to
show him those who were shorter

00:11:40.630 --> 00:11:42.518
than six foot and made it?

00:11:42.518 --> 00:11:44.450
- He's all about, he
thinks he's Steph Curry.

00:11:44.450 --> 00:11:47.810
He already knows who Spud
Webb is, of course, there is.

00:11:47.810 --> 00:11:49.750
- But he's also using what he has.

00:11:49.750 --> 00:11:51.780
So he's using his
ability, if he can't dunk,

00:11:51.780 --> 00:11:52.613
then he's like gonna--

00:11:52.613 --> 00:11:54.143
- Well, it's really interesting

00:11:54.143 --> 00:11:54.990
it's like all I talk to him about

00:11:54.990 --> 00:11:57.400
is jump shooting and left hands.

00:11:57.400 --> 00:11:59.300
Like if he's gonna be any prayer

00:11:59.300 --> 00:12:02.470
of like a decent D3 basketball player,

00:12:02.470 --> 00:12:04.610
like he better fucking shoot his ass off

00:12:04.610 --> 00:12:06.615
and better have a ridiculous left hand.

00:12:06.615 --> 00:12:07.919
You know what I mean,
like you know so like

00:12:07.919 --> 00:12:10.070
it's just this conversation

00:12:10.070 --> 00:12:12.410
which is like do not create delusion.

00:12:12.410 --> 00:12:14.470
However, what caught my attention

00:12:14.470 --> 00:12:16.167
when you were going there is
when your parents tell you

00:12:16.167 --> 00:12:19.700
you can do anything
everybody who's accomplished

00:12:19.700 --> 00:12:22.720
something remarkable
basically either had that

00:12:22.720 --> 00:12:26.830
or such the reverse that
they were like fuck you

00:12:26.830 --> 00:12:27.680
that it drove them.

00:12:27.680 --> 00:12:29.990
But that person tends to get there

00:12:29.990 --> 00:12:32.097
and be unhappy versus the
person that, you know,

00:12:32.097 --> 00:12:34.080
and so there's a positive framework.

00:12:34.080 --> 00:12:35.430
Nonetheless, how about you?

00:12:36.300 --> 00:12:38.650
- My state where I grew
up, you're asking how?

00:12:38.650 --> 00:12:39.883
- Yeah, what kind of kid were you?

00:12:39.883 --> 00:12:43.410
I love origins, I'm really
big on comic book number one.

00:12:43.410 --> 00:12:45.290
Like the next series of questions for me

00:12:45.290 --> 00:12:48.247
is gonna be predicated on
where did you grow up, how?

00:12:48.247 --> 00:12:49.860
You know like it helps me understand

00:12:49.860 --> 00:12:51.630
how you got to "The Stuff", it helps me,

00:12:51.630 --> 00:12:53.253
it helps Andy pick the
next couple of questions.

00:12:53.253 --> 00:12:55.250
I'm fascinated with context.

00:12:55.250 --> 00:12:56.083
- [Sampson] Right, right, got you.

00:12:56.083 --> 00:12:57.840
- So where were you born and
what kind of kid were you?

00:12:57.840 --> 00:12:59.848
- Yeah, I mean I was born in New Jersey.

00:12:59.848 --> 00:13:00.681
- [Gary] Where?

00:13:00.681 --> 00:13:01.514
- Norton, New Jersey.

00:13:01.514 --> 00:13:02.347
- [Gary] I love it.

00:13:02.347 --> 00:13:03.180
- Yeah, Norton, New Jersey.

00:13:03.180 --> 00:13:04.013
- [Gary] I grew up in Edison.

00:13:04.013 --> 00:13:04.846
- Okay, oh yeah, I went
to school at Rutgers,

00:13:04.846 --> 00:13:06.181
they're not too far.

00:13:06.181 --> 00:13:07.014
- [Gary] I love it, very close.

00:13:07.014 --> 00:13:09.460
- Yeah, so but I was--
- You were a kid.

00:13:09.460 --> 00:13:11.070
- [Sampson] I was just an ambitious kid.

00:13:11.070 --> 00:13:13.700
You know what, I think I just
had this kind of inner drive.

00:13:13.700 --> 00:13:14.670
- [Gary] How old are you?

00:13:14.670 --> 00:13:15.662
- When I thought that way?

00:13:15.662 --> 00:13:16.495
- [Gary] Right now.

00:13:16.495 --> 00:13:17.328
- Oh, 46.

00:13:17.328 --> 00:13:18.161
- [Gary] Okay, keep going.

00:13:18.161 --> 00:13:19.480
- So I had this inner drive to just really

00:13:19.480 --> 00:13:21.930
just become something in life.

00:13:21.930 --> 00:13:25.270
I can't really explain it, I
just really believed in life

00:13:25.270 --> 00:13:26.340
that I can do it.

00:13:26.340 --> 00:13:28.700
I grew up single-parent
home with five siblings

00:13:28.700 --> 00:13:30.720
but it was a challenging environment,

00:13:30.720 --> 00:13:32.270
but at the same time like I knew

00:13:32.270 --> 00:13:33.490
that I wanted to be something.

00:13:33.490 --> 00:13:35.670
Now, what that something
was I had no clue.

00:13:35.670 --> 00:13:37.920
Of course, I would have
loved to play baseball

00:13:37.920 --> 00:13:39.717
but you had that position
was already taken already.

00:13:39.717 --> 00:13:41.328
(he laughs)

00:13:41.328 --> 00:13:43.090
- Were you a sports kid?

00:13:43.090 --> 00:13:44.750
- Yeah, I played baseball, yeah.

00:13:44.750 --> 00:13:46.670
I played baseball, basketball.

00:13:46.670 --> 00:13:47.610
- So he says, Gary.

00:13:47.610 --> 00:13:48.530
- So I say.
(Sharlee laughs)

00:13:48.530 --> 00:13:50.800
I have a few articles in
a small paper somewhere.

00:13:50.800 --> 00:13:52.880
- Were you good at school?

00:13:52.880 --> 00:13:53.713
- Yeah, I was.

00:13:53.713 --> 00:13:56.250
I was, school came easy.

00:13:56.250 --> 00:13:58.240
You know like I mean it was
something that I enjoyed doing

00:13:58.240 --> 00:14:00.598
but it wasn't a cool or
popular thing, right.

00:14:00.598 --> 00:14:01.638
(he laughs)

00:14:01.638 --> 00:14:03.268
- I'm 43, I came from Jersey,

00:14:03.268 --> 00:14:04.123
I know what the vibe was at the time.

00:14:04.123 --> 00:14:06.805
- Right, right so you know
you would always sort of hide

00:14:06.805 --> 00:14:08.780
or not really brag about that.

00:14:08.780 --> 00:14:10.153
- [Gary] Of course, you would
downplay how good you were.

00:14:10.153 --> 00:14:11.364
- Yeah, yeah.

00:14:11.364 --> 00:14:12.197
- Were good at school?

00:14:12.197 --> 00:14:13.030
Like nah, I got a C in.

00:14:13.030 --> 00:14:14.200
- Right, right, yeah, yeah.

00:14:14.200 --> 00:14:15.870
No, like I got a 95, right.

00:14:15.870 --> 00:14:17.170
(Gary laughs)

00:14:17.170 --> 00:14:20.100
And so, it just was this
drive to become something

00:14:20.100 --> 00:14:22.930
and to achieve something in life.

00:14:22.930 --> 00:14:24.760
And just kind of moving along

00:14:24.760 --> 00:14:27.370
and as life took me it
just became an opportunity

00:14:27.370 --> 00:14:29.343
or opportunity presented
itself to go to medical school

00:14:29.343 --> 00:14:30.720
and to become a doctor.

00:14:30.720 --> 00:14:32.920
Now, I didn't know what
the hell a doctor did

00:14:32.920 --> 00:14:35.670
besides pediatricians and
giving shots and everything.

00:14:35.670 --> 00:14:37.730
I didn't know the details
and so I just went for it

00:14:37.730 --> 00:14:39.120
because it was something more than

00:14:39.120 --> 00:14:40.433
what I had in front of me.

00:14:41.300 --> 00:14:43.260
- It intuitively felt like
a proper step forward.

00:14:43.260 --> 00:14:44.780
- Right and I enjoyed helping people

00:14:44.780 --> 00:14:46.870
and I will always have a job.

00:14:46.870 --> 00:14:47.825
So let me go for it.

00:14:47.825 --> 00:14:48.658
(he laughs)

00:14:48.658 --> 00:14:52.582
- Understood and how,
and in that career path

00:14:52.582 --> 00:14:55.360
have you maintained the same passion?

00:14:55.360 --> 00:14:58.910
You know obviously you
know speaking and writing,

00:14:58.910 --> 00:15:00.600
your scratching other things

00:15:00.600 --> 00:15:03.170
that you know you're passionate about.

00:15:03.170 --> 00:15:06.820
You know a lot of people
watch and listen to this

00:15:07.865 --> 00:15:11.000
are attracted to my
message because I think,

00:15:11.000 --> 00:15:14.013
back to you can do anything
but it has to be practical,

00:15:14.013 --> 00:15:17.274
what has happened you know three
of us are far closer in age

00:15:17.274 --> 00:15:19.430
than a lot of people in this room

00:15:19.430 --> 00:15:20.900
and a lot of people that are watching.

00:15:20.900 --> 00:15:24.200
We did not grow up with a mature internet

00:15:24.200 --> 00:15:27.110
that actually allowed us
to have unlimited options

00:15:28.121 --> 00:15:31.400
in perpetuity around our core interests

00:15:31.400 --> 00:15:33.370
and that is what's happening now.

00:15:33.370 --> 00:15:35.960
So, I guess the reason
I'm asking you that is

00:15:35.960 --> 00:15:37.110
I think there's a lot of
people who are watching

00:15:37.110 --> 00:15:40.230
who ebb and flow in the
passion of the career choice

00:15:40.230 --> 00:15:42.830
they've given and when they're in the dip

00:15:42.830 --> 00:15:43.663
they're thinking about

00:15:43.663 --> 00:15:46.080
should I start my side hustle or thing.

00:15:46.080 --> 00:15:49.020
Have you know, I'm just very
curious about what you're doing

00:15:49.020 --> 00:15:50.993
with this and what your career is.

00:15:50.993 --> 00:15:53.040
It's a very fun question for me to ask.

00:15:53.040 --> 00:15:56.320
Have you maintained that love for it?

00:15:56.320 --> 00:15:57.670
Has it never fully been there

00:15:57.670 --> 00:16:00.300
because it was the opportunity to win

00:16:00.300 --> 00:16:02.870
and advance has it ebbed and flowed?

00:16:02.870 --> 00:16:07.480
How is your love for
that profession navigated

00:16:07.480 --> 00:16:08.340
over the last 20 years?

00:16:08.340 --> 00:16:09.942
- Yeah, that's a very good question.

00:16:09.942 --> 00:16:12.600
I think it does or it
has ebbed and flowed,

00:16:12.600 --> 00:16:15.320
in the sense that I enjoy medicine

00:16:15.320 --> 00:16:16.520
but I also enjoy speaking.

00:16:16.520 --> 00:16:18.110
I also enjoy writing books.

00:16:18.110 --> 00:16:20.310
Like this is the 5th
book that I've written.

00:16:21.512 --> 00:16:26.512
So, I enjoy the
progression of what's next.

00:16:26.640 --> 00:16:28.550
The evolution which is unknown.

00:16:28.550 --> 00:16:29.500
Because you really don't know.

00:16:29.500 --> 00:16:32.430
You know your passion is to
achieve and to be successful

00:16:32.430 --> 00:16:35.510
and it might be within a career
aspect of what you're doing

00:16:35.510 --> 00:16:38.220
but at the same time I
enjoy the aspect of life.

00:16:38.220 --> 00:16:41.420
Of seeing what sort of presents
itself as the next thing.

00:16:41.420 --> 00:16:42.403
You don't often know what that is.

00:16:42.403 --> 00:16:44.400
But you just kind of follow the flow

00:16:44.400 --> 00:16:47.810
of just applying your formula of success

00:16:47.810 --> 00:16:49.110
and seeing it grow and develop.

00:16:49.110 --> 00:16:52.130
So, yeah, practicing medicine
and being an ER physician

00:16:52.130 --> 00:16:54.550
and saving lives and
rescuing people from--

00:16:54.550 --> 00:16:56.140
- How'd it feel the first
time to save somebody's life?

00:16:56.140 --> 00:16:57.320
- Oh, it's amazing.

00:16:57.320 --> 00:16:58.970
It's like you have your own theme music.

00:16:58.970 --> 00:17:00.298
You walk out and it's like--

00:17:00.298 --> 00:17:01.131
- Talking about something
I could never do,

00:17:01.131 --> 00:17:04.170
the second I would see
any level of like trauma

00:17:04.170 --> 00:17:06.868
and blood I would literally faint.

00:17:06.868 --> 00:17:08.620
(all laugh)

00:17:08.620 --> 00:17:09.453
Seriously.

00:17:09.453 --> 00:17:10.286
- [Sampson] And you know what--

00:17:10.286 --> 00:17:12.270
- I just walk into hospitals
sometimes and get lightheaded.

00:17:12.270 --> 00:17:14.360
Walk in the front door of the hospital.

00:17:14.360 --> 00:17:15.341
- So this is the beauty.

00:17:15.341 --> 00:17:17.066
(all laugh)

00:17:17.066 --> 00:17:18.270
But, yeah, the beauty about life is like

00:17:18.270 --> 00:17:19.719
the first time I saw a surgery

00:17:19.719 --> 00:17:22.480
I lost my lunch in the scrub sink.

00:17:22.480 --> 00:17:23.313
- [Gary] You did?

00:17:23.313 --> 00:17:24.443
- Yeah, and so I'm in college

00:17:24.443 --> 00:17:26.254
and I'm like okay I
chose the wrong career.

00:17:26.254 --> 00:17:28.398
I like shit like how I'm
gonna you know fix this?

00:17:28.398 --> 00:17:29.698
- And did like everybody else laugh

00:17:29.698 --> 00:17:31.990
like it was a right of passage?

00:17:31.990 --> 00:17:33.095
Did like some mentor come to you

00:17:33.095 --> 00:17:34.280
and be like, don't worry I did that, too?

00:17:34.280 --> 00:17:36.740
Or they were just laughing at
you or how did that go down?

00:17:36.740 --> 00:17:38.510
- The nurse is, "You gotta
go, you gotta get out."

00:17:38.510 --> 00:17:39.720
She kicked me out of the OR.

00:17:39.720 --> 00:17:40.923
- [Gary] There's no
joking around in the OR.

00:17:40.923 --> 00:17:42.011
I get that.

00:17:42.011 --> 00:17:43.920
- Yeah, but that's what I'm saying

00:17:43.920 --> 00:17:45.546
like this is what I think sometimes

00:17:45.546 --> 00:17:49.700
hinder us as people is
that we go after something

00:17:49.700 --> 00:17:51.820
we believe in and we
sort of receive a message

00:17:51.820 --> 00:17:53.720
that this isn't for me
or it's too challenging.

00:17:53.720 --> 00:17:55.493
- A micro adversity we take it as a macro.

00:17:55.493 --> 00:17:57.330
- Right, that's right.

00:17:57.330 --> 00:17:59.140
You grow this monster in your mind

00:17:59.140 --> 00:18:00.897
to be bigger than what it
is and you don't pursue it

00:18:00.897 --> 00:18:04.070
and then, boom it's like now
you're not reaching your level.

00:18:04.070 --> 00:18:05.066
- Can we talk about this?

00:18:05.066 --> 00:18:05.899
I don't know if you cover it in the book.

00:18:05.899 --> 00:18:07.070
Actually there's two things.

00:18:07.070 --> 00:18:08.650
Two things, one if you got phone numbers,

00:18:08.650 --> 00:18:10.490
YouTube, Facebook we're about to do calls.

00:18:10.490 --> 00:18:13.000
Two, I want you to give
me five bullet points

00:18:13.000 --> 00:18:14.240
of like takeaways for the book

00:18:14.240 --> 00:18:15.890
'cause I want everybody to buy it.

00:18:15.890 --> 00:18:18.140
But before we get into that,

00:18:18.140 --> 00:18:22.240
you know we're here because you

00:18:22.240 --> 00:18:24.220
and I have hung out a
couple of times and we vibed

00:18:24.220 --> 00:18:25.960
and I'm like, oh shit, we
see the world the same way.

00:18:25.960 --> 00:18:28.610
So I've been excited about
this but excited to meet you.

00:18:28.610 --> 00:18:30.140
So I'm leading the
witness a little bit here

00:18:30.140 --> 00:18:35.140
but this micro failure as a
macro, how much do you think

00:18:36.410 --> 00:18:39.150
the current state of demonizing failure

00:18:39.150 --> 00:18:43.323
in our society early on
leads to enormous issues

00:18:44.640 --> 00:18:46.990
or vulnerabilities to
what we're referring to

00:18:46.990 --> 00:18:48.800
Like do you, let me ask
it in a different way.

00:18:48.800 --> 00:18:51.300
Do you believe that we need to start

00:18:51.300 --> 00:18:55.580
beating a drum of, especially
at a parenting level,

00:18:55.580 --> 00:18:59.040
to start really embracing
failure much earlier

00:18:59.040 --> 00:19:02.455
which then won't make somebody
who loses their lunch.

00:19:02.455 --> 00:19:06.110
There are actual people on
Earth who lost their lunch

00:19:06.110 --> 00:19:08.450
put in all that time and
effort into medical school,

00:19:08.450 --> 00:19:11.580
lost their lunch, went
home, called mommy and daddy

